    "updated_at": "2025-10-19T03:04:42Z",
    "body": "Hi @annevk — thank you for responding!\n\nYou’re right that HTML already supports basic declarative validation via type, required, and pattern.\nMy suggestion is to extend that concept to support higher-level, real-world validation patterns that currently require custom JavaScript.\n\nFor example, fields like:\n\n- validation=\"confirm\" → confirm password matching another input\n- validation=\"password\" → native password strength check\n- validation=\"phone\" → standardized international phone format\n- validation=\"name\" → simple alphabetic name validation\n\nExample usage:  \n<input type=\"text\" validation=\"phone\">\n\nThese are extremely common across the web, but currently every developer must implement them manually with JS or third-party libraries.\n\nThe idea is not to replace type or pattern, but to propose a browser-native, semantic, and standardized validation attribute that covers these advanced cases consistently — similar in spirit to how type=\"email\" simplified email validation.\n\nIt could help make forms:\n\n- Lighter (no JS dependencies)\n- More consistent across browsers\n- Easier for beginners\n- More accessible (using built-in error messaging)\n\n\nDemo for reference:\nhttps://mtechamit.github.io/adaptive-form-validation/\n\nGitHub: https://github.com/mtechamit/adaptive-form-validation\n\nThis is my humble request — I would really appreciate it if the team could consider adding something like this. \n\nThanks again for your time! I’d love to refine this idea further based on your guidance.",
